  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periodic, scattered to occasionally more numerous \nshowers and thunderstorms expected through Friday night.\n\nCyclonic flow overhead of the region today will allow embedded \nshortwave troughs to pass across New York State through tonight. \nMeanwhile at the surface a secondary cold front is gradually sagging \nsouth early this morning and should reach the NY/PA state line by \ndaybreak. As the front moves south this morning, there will remain \nchances for a few additional showers, though these showers will \nremain south of the I-90. \n\nSome additional scattered showers and storms will be possible later \ntoday for areas southeast of the Niagara Frontier towards the \nwestern Southern Tier due to the vicinity of the lingering frontal \nboundary as it continues to gradually push south and west today. \n\nSurface high pressure associated with mid-level ridging across the \nGreat Lakes, will support fair dry weather to return tonight. With \nthe surface high building overhead, stabilizing subsidence will \nbecome more extensive and strengthen across western and northcentral \nNew York. Clearing skies and lingering moisture and radiational \ncooling will support fog to develop across much of the Finger Lakes \narea.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Quieter weather expected this Labor Day weekend.\n\nMid-level ridge and corresponding surface high pressure will ridge \nacross the Great Lakes this weekend, where associated dry air and \nsubsidence will support a drying trend for the holiday weekend. In \naddition to the dry air, temperatures will be 10 degrees cooler than \nthe past couple of days, supporting highs to be near normal to \nslightly below normal for the start of September. \n\nClear skies beneath the surface high, the cooler airmass overhead, \nweak surface winds, along with lingering low level moisture will \nsupport some valley steam fog to develop each night across areas \neast of Lake Ontario, the western Southern Tier and into the Finger \nLakes.",
